## Why this topic matters

Intellectual property law is the legal frame around every creative or
technical output that a digital business produces. Code, design assets,
brand identity, written content, music in apps, training data for
machine-learning models, and the look and feel of a user interface all
sit inside one or more IP regimes. A Paper 1 scenario that involves
copying, reusing, distributing, or commercially exploiting somebody
else's creative or technical work usually points at IP law as the lead
Act.

The most common student error in this area is treating IP as a single
unified Act. It is not. Several distinct regimes apply to different
kinds of subject matter, and the first move in any IP scenario is to
identify which regime is engaged.

## What IP actually is

UK intellectual property law is not one statute but a set of regimes,
each with its own subject matter, governing statute, formal
requirements and enforcement route.

- **Copyright.** Protects original creative works. Governed by the
  Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988. Arises automatically on
  creation; no registration required.
- **Patents.** Protect new inventions. Governed by the Patents Act
  1977. Must be applied for and granted by the UK Intellectual
  Property Office.
- **Trade marks.** Protect signs (names, logos, slogans, certain
  sounds) used in trade. Governed by the Trade Marks Act 1994. Must
  be registered for full protection, although an "unregistered" common
  law right exists through the tort of passing off.
- **Designs.** Protect the appearance of a product. Governed by the
  Registered Designs Act 1949 (for registered designs) and Part III
  of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 (for unregistered
  design right).
- **Database right.** A separate right in databases that involve
  substantial investment, sitting alongside copyright. Governed by
  the Copyright and Rights in Databases Regulations 1997.

Most Paper 1 scenarios sit inside copyright or, less often, trade
marks. The other regimes appear at the edges of the syllabus.

## Copyright in detail (CDPA 1988)

Copyright is the dominant IP regime for digital scenarios because the
Act treats computer software, databases and digital creative content
as protectable works.

### What copyright protects

Section 1 of the CDPA 1988 lists the categories of work that copyright
protects: original literary works, original dramatic works, original
musical works, original artistic works, sound recordings, films,
broadcasts, and the typographical arrangement of published editions.
Software is treated as a literary work under section 3. Databases that
meet the originality threshold are also literary works, in addition to
the separate database right that sits alongside copyright.

The threshold for "originality" in copyright is low. The work must
originate with the author and involve a measure of independent
intellectual effort, but it does not need to be novel in the patent
sense or be artistically valuable.

### How copyright arises

Copyright arises automatically the moment an original work is fixed in
a material form (saved to disk, recorded, drawn on paper, or otherwise
captured in a way that persists beyond the moment of creation). No
registration, deposit, publication or marking is required for
copyright to exist or be enforceable in the UK. The (c) symbol is
informative for readers but legally optional.

### Who owns the copyright

Section 11 sets out the first-ownership rule. The first owner of
copyright in a work is the author (the person who created the work),
with one major exception: where the work is created by an employee in
the course of their employment, the first owner is the employer.

For commissioned freelance work, the freelancer is the first owner of
copyright in the work they created, even where they have been paid.
The commissioner gets only an implied licence to use the work for the
purpose for which it was commissioned, unless an express written
assignment is in place. This is the single most common IP mistake in
small digital businesses, and it appears regularly in Paper 1
scenarios.

### How copyright can be transferred or licensed

Section 90 requires an assignment of copyright to be in writing and
signed by the assignor. A verbal agreement plus payment is not enough.
Licences can be granted on more flexible terms and can be exclusive or
non-exclusive, but the underlying ownership only transfers in writing.

### What copyright protects against

Section 16 sets out the acts restricted by copyright: copying the
work, issuing copies to the public, renting or lending the work to the
public, performing or playing the work in public, communicating the
work to the public, and making an adaptation of the work. Doing any of
these without licence amounts to primary infringement. Secondary
infringement (such as importing or dealing with infringing copies for
commercial purposes) is also actionable.

There are limited statutory exceptions ("fair dealing" for purposes
such as research and private study, criticism and review, news
reporting, quotation, parody and pastiche; plus specific exceptions
for educational use and for backup copies of software) but these are
narrower than students often assume.

### Duration

For literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works, copyright lasts
for the life of the author plus 70 years from the end of the calendar
year in which the author died. Films, sound recordings and broadcasts
have shorter rules.

## Patents (Patents Act 1977)

The Patents Act 1977 protects new inventions through a registration
system administered by the UK Intellectual Property Office. A
patentable invention must be new, involve an inventive step over the
prior art, be capable of industrial application, and not fall into one
of the excluded categories under section 1(2). Patent protection
lasts up to 20 years from filing, subject to renewal fees.

The category most relevant to T-Level Digital is the exclusion of
"programs for computers" under section 1(2)(c). A computer program
"as such" is not patentable. However, the UK and European courts have
developed a body of case law that allows patenting of inventions that
happen to be implemented in software, provided the invention makes a
technical contribution beyond the program itself (a faster image
compression algorithm with a measurable hardware effect; a control
system for a physical process). The "as such" qualifier does
substantial work in this exclusion, and the answer to "is software
patentable" is "usually no, sometimes yes, depending on the technical
contribution".

For Paper 1, the safe Name is "computer software is generally
protected by copyright under the CDPA 1988, not by patents under the
Patents Act 1977, because section 1(2)(c) excludes programs for
computers as such from patentability". Software-and-patents nuance
beyond that is unlikely to be tested.

## Trade marks (Trade Marks Act 1994)

The Trade Marks Act 1994 protects signs (names, logos, slogans and
certain sounds or shapes) used in the course of trade. Registration
with the UK Intellectual Property Office grants a 10-year protection
that can be renewed indefinitely. Unregistered marks have a separate
common law right enforceable through the tort of passing off, but the
evidential burden is much higher than for registered marks.

For digital scenarios, trade marks bite when a competitor uses a name
or logo that is confusingly similar to a registered mark. Domain
names, app store listings, social media handles and brand identities
are typical trade-mark territory.

## Other IP regimes (brief)

- **Designs.** Protect the appearance of a product, registered or
  unregistered. The registered design right under the Registered
  Designs Act 1949 lasts up to 25 years; unregistered design right
  under Part III of the CDPA 1988 lasts shorter.
- **Database right.** A separate right under the Copyright and Rights
  in Databases Regulations 1997. Protects the substantial investment
  in obtaining, verifying or presenting the contents of a database.
  Lasts 15 years from completion, renewed by substantial new
  investment.
- **Trade secrets.** Protected by a mix of common law (breach of
  confidence) and the Trade Secrets (Enforcement, etc.) Regulations
  2018. Distinct from copyright in that secrecy is the substance of
  the protection rather than originality.

## Open source and digital practice

The single most common IP misunderstanding among new developers is the
belief that "open source" means "no rules". It does not. Open-source
software is copyrighted by its authors and is released to the public
under a licence that grants permission to use, modify and redistribute
the software on specific terms. The licence is enforceable as a
copyright licence: using the software in a way that breaches the
licence terms amounts to copyright infringement.

The two broad families of open-source licence are:

- **Permissive licences** (MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0). Allow use,
  modification and redistribution including in proprietary commercial
  products, subject to lightweight conditions like preserving the
  copyright notice.
- **Copyleft licences** (GPL, AGPL, LGPL to a degree). Require that
  derivative works are also released under the same licence. The GPL
  in particular requires source code of the derivative work to be
  made available. Shipping a closed-source commercial product that
  incorporates GPL software, without complying with the source-release
  obligation, is copyright infringement.

For Paper 1, the safe Name is "open-source software is copyrighted
under the CDPA 1988 and licensed on terms that must be complied with;
non-compliance is copyright infringement".

## Enforcement and consequences

IP rights are enforced through civil proceedings in the High Court (or
the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court for lower-value claims).
Available remedies include injunctions to stop further infringement,
damages or an account of the infringer's profits, delivery up or
destruction of infringing items, and orders to make a public statement
correcting any misrepresentation in trade-mark or passing-off cases.

Some IP offences are also criminal. Copyright piracy on a commercial
scale (CDPA section 107) and unauthorised use of a registered trade
mark on goods (Trade Marks Act section 92) can attract criminal
prosecution, with penalties including imprisonment for the most
serious cases. For the typical Paper 1 scenario, however, civil
liability is the lead consequence.

Beyond legal liability, IP disputes carry reputational consequences
that are often more commercially damaging than the legal outcome.
Public exposure of an open-source licence violation can destroy a
company's standing with the developer community. Trade-mark disputes
visibly play out in app store listings and search results. Copyright
disputes with freelance creators often go public on social media.

## What Paper 1 expects

When a Paper 1 scenario shows somebody copying, reusing, distributing
or commissioning creative or technical work, the path through the
answer is:

1. **Name the regime and the Act.** "Copyright under the Copyright,
   Designs and Patents Act 1988" for almost every digital case
   involving original creative or software content. Trade marks if the
   scenario is about a name, logo or brand identity in trade. Patents
   only where the scenario is explicit about a registered patent.

2. **Explain the relevant rule.** State the test for protection (for
   copyright, originality plus fixation; no registration required),
   identify who owns the right (section 11 for copyright first
   ownership, with the employee exception), and state what the right
   protects against (section 16 restricted acts).

3. **Apply the rule to the scenario.** Tie the test to the facts. The
   strongest answers identify the work, name the rightsholder, name
   the infringer, and identify the specific act of infringement. For
   commissioned-work
   scenarios, name the section 11 default and the section 90 written
   assignment requirement.

The recurring trap is the assumption that registration is required for
copyright to exist, or that "open source" means "no rules". Both
assumptions are wrong, and scenarios that turn on either are common.

The worked examples in the Method section show this path running on
past-paper questions. Once you have practised the assessment below,
come back to those.
